e  eighth grade schools classes so we  didn't really want to lose that audience  and we thought if we went The Kite  Runner route it would be a little more  inaccessible and penber  in making it live action you know some  of the scenes would be that much harder  to take and a little harder to introduce  young people to it so we thought if we  went animated and one phrase that Nora  to me the director coined early on was  you know it allows us to kind of sugar  the pill when we're dealing with some of  the harsher realities of the situation  with animation so you know we looked at  animated films that really stood out to  us that had a really strong artistic  point of view and looked at films like  Persepolis and The Secret of Kells which  cartoon saloon had done so they  literally were the first people on our  list and we met with them and and talked  about Co producing the film together and  and they said yes and the rest is kind  of history you guys have made a lot of  music for a lot of movies both  live-action and animation even your  animation stuff's been more the  commercial Pixar and Warner Brothers  kind of thing how did what was the  challenges of this particular story this  required a different sort of thing than  it it definitely did and and when we  first met with Nora she said that you  know the mandate for the music was to in  the same way that the pill was being  sugared story-wise and visually that  musically she really wanted you know  true authentic Afghan music to be part  of this story it's it's their story she  wanted to hear their voices you know but  also to combine that with Western music  and make make that kind of Afghan music  accessible and and emotionally  accessible for Western audiences  audiences internationally it's something  that would help tell the story but  really give us a sense of place so our  big challenge so Jeff Ann and I when we  began our studying of Afghan music and  the great thing about animation is you  have this great lead time so we've  literally were working on it a year  before our final recording so we have on  and off but we had that all that time to  learn  about Afghan music to make our contacts  which ended up to be extremely difficult  because of the Taliban's their mandate  to destroy art basically and destroy  music then in the time that they ruled  Afghanistan and Kabul  they succeeded in wiping out you know  all music schools all schools all  education for girls musicians were  scattered stopped playing so the  tradition kind of got broken in a sense  and it's already a very complicated  tradition because of the tumultuous  history of the land there's kind of this  melange of Persian and Indian and and  then there's all the tribes and they all  have their own kind of music so it was a  real challenge for Jeff and I to kind of  wrap our heads around it get into it and  then to find players and so there's very  few in North America our original plan  was to go to Kabul to record how the  situation had deteriorated badly kind of  kind of last year so we ended up having  to figure out a way to record the those  Afghan instruments remotely and we made  contact with this school in Afghanistan  called the Afghanistan National  Institute of Music  this is a school that was begun in the  days after the Taliban where they not  only teach music but they also teach  girls it's a co-ed school so we were  able to get in work our way into that  kind of world and record remotely the  music shifts a little for the  storytelling sequences right you want to  talk about the difference yeah it was  apparent right away when we saw the film  with Nora the first time that the film  travels down two parallel tracks one is  a real-world as she called it and story  world the beautiful thing is at the end  they merge and they become one and we  understand that the story world  sequences are all explaining the subtext  for real world spoke we realized that  there we needed a slightly different  approach for both those things as we  move forward in the film so real world  was a little more prosaic sombre  stripped-down not as big an ensemble not  as  floral and approach nor even had this  thing where she didn't want us to finish  any the exposition of any of the themes  in the first two reels she said you've  written these beautiful themes now I  only want to hear half of it when we  first meet parwana  which was new for us we you know we'd  never sort of done it she's I could cut  it off halfway like her story's not done  I don't want the music story done which  was an interesting approach and it  worked out quite well but so that was  this sort of truncated and small sound  for real world and then store will  conversely very colorful even more the  Afghan sounds and players followed it  closer to picture a little bit more of  the animated style of scoring because  there's some gymnastics and and some a  lot of fun going on in those those  popping store world scenes and then they  come to slowly you know store well gets  a bit darker as the story goes on real  world gets a bit bigger as she goes to  find she goes to the prison and then it  comes all together right there at the  end let's talk about the director who  isn't here with us today  Norah how was she selected I know that  cartoon saloon is a relatively new  studio right relatively it took ten  years or more I guess and they made two  features yeah their previous two  features were the Secret of Kells and  song at the scene which were directed by  Tom Moore one of the producers here yes  so how did how did Nora get involved  with this sure well the the great thing  about cartoon saloon is they're you know  their studio artists the three  principals Paul Young Tom Moore and  Norah to me all come from animation  backgrounds and artists themselves so it  was never really set up to be just a  vehicle for Tom's work so in this  instance we when we approached cartoon  saloon we talked about you know the  story and kind of where we wanted to do  with it and Nora I guess it got in the  book and had gone home and read it in  one night because she was just she was  so captivated by the story and and what  she could do with it or mind was racing  and so she came back and pitched us all  on this idea of her directing and and  how she would do it and everything and  it was pretty unanimous decisions just  that she was going to be the ones to do  it so you're hoping everyone people  around  world families will see this movie and  can you speak to that like what you hope  they'll they'll get out of it because  there's a lot you can get out of this  movie you can you learn about the  experience of this family but but you  know also about the world and there's so  much you can talk about yeah I mean for  I know that you know we never set out to  do a movie with a message or a political  film or anything like that Nora was  always very adamant about you know this  is a young girl's story it's about her  it's about her family it's about you  know getting her father back  you know universal themes that everyone  can relate to and it's been really  rewarding seeing different people watch  the film and you know a lot of Afghan  people have watched the film and nothing  but praise for I hope is the more people  see it you know the more it broadens  this discussion and adds kind of a  counter-argument this is me personally  speaking like just to some of the  arguments out there now for against  diversity you know in this world so you  know we always welcome the chance to do  much of what was just there musically  and to take an opportunity with a  musical culture like Afghanistan to  bring into the spotlight a little bit  the music and the musicians that don't  usually get certainly in a Hollywood  censor and in US large scope sense a  real fair look and there's some amazing  and beautiful music all over the world  that's rarely heard so this was a great  opportunity for us to bring forth music  musicians particularly we're excited to  work with the this choir Mike alluded to  that dr. SAR mast knew about these  little girls who were part one as age  that we got to record from us just a  small school choir they weren't pros it  doesn't sound like an Anglican Church  Choir you know it's not beautiful but  it's just the perfect rough-hewn sound  for our film and that was a session we  did via Skype in the middle of the night  for us  it was during Ramadan for them so they  were fasting and they had to keep  sitting down so it was it was kind about  was one of those moments you talk about  what what's different about it in this  the score is really to be able to raise  their culture up a little bit into the  spotlight was we were grateful to be  able to do that  yeah and it's a privilege to be  to work on a film that just you know if  we have any hope it's it's that we all  on this planet develop a sense of  empathy and understanding for other  people and this film is so personal it's  a personal story and people it's not  people over there it's it's a your it's  a family and with challenges that you  you can relate to when you see them in  this film so it was such a privilege for  us to be able to work on something that  really you know has such a positive  ability to impact people and and build  that bridge and musically that was  something that was really important to  us that we do bring east and west  together like that I want to thank all  you guys amazing movie

